\(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MgO}\) ,[24] \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) ,[25] \(\mathrm{MgO/TiO}_{2}\) ,[17] \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{ZnO}\) ,[26] and \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) .[27] In contrast, the \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of PSCs generally depends to a large extent on the energy level of the ETL for a specific bandgap material. Well-matched energy alignment between the ETL and perovskite films enhances charge transfer, and reduces interfacial recombination for efficient PSCs.[28] Therefore, controlling the energy level of the ETL is also beneficial for improving the performance of PSCs. However, planar devices are still less efficient than mesoporous devices.

Here, we introduce a simple low-temperature solution-processed \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) bilayer ETL for efficient planar PSCs. We demonstrate that uniform and pinhole-free perovskite films can be obtained by the introduction of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) . The band energy alignment can be also tailored with increased built-in potential, resulting in an enhanced electric field for the separation of carriers and enhanced charge transfer, as well as reduced carrier recombination. The planar PSC utilizing \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) ETL demonstrated a higher efficiency of \(23.24\%\) with negligible hysteresis and \(22.74\%\) stable power output compared with a \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) - based device that has an efficiency of \(21.42\%\) . In addition, the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) -based PSC maintains \(97.5\%\) of its initial PCE after being stored under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) for 80 days without encapsulation and

retain \(91\%\) of their initial PCE after \(180~\mathrm{h}\) under 1 sun continuous illumination.

Figure 1a–d shows the surface morphologies of ITO, ITO/ \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) , \(\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) , and \(\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) , respectively. Compared with bare ITO, the surface of \(\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) is evidently much smoother. Moreover, \(\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) has an improved roughness and much better homogeneity than that of ITO/ \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) . Atom force microscopy (AFM) was conducted to further investigate the roughness of the samples. As shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information), the root mean square (RMS) roughness of these four samples was reduced from \(5.5\ \mathrm{nm}\) for ITO only to \(2.2\ \mathrm{nm}\) after spin-coating \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) . The roughness of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) spin-coated on bare ITO decreased from 2.2 to \(1.4~\mathrm{nm}\) when \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) was introduced. X-ray diffraction (XRD) and scanning electron microscopy (SEM) verified the existence of the thin \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) layer. As shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information), there are distinct diffraction peaks at \(30.6^{\circ}\) , \(35.5^{\circ}\) , and \(51.0^{\circ}\) , indicating the formation of pure \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) crystals. The cross-sectional SEM image of the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) film further confirmed its thickness of \({\approx}10\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure S3, Supporting Information). \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) nanoparticles do not easily form dense films on rough ITO, but sol–gel-processed \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) may enhance the physical
contact with ITO, improving the electrical performance of resulting devices.[29]

To further demonstrate the influence of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), top-view SEM images of the perovskite films were examined. As shown in Figure 1e,f, uniform and pinhole-free perovskite films with larger grain sizes can be obtained when deposited on the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) ETL substrate than on \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) ETL. The grain size of perovskite films is increased by an average of around \(200\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure S4, Supporting Information) with the incorporation of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\). Pinholes on the surface of the films will reduce the shunt resistance and lead to recombination,[30] which are detrimental to the performance of devices. Moreover, the RMS roughness of corresponding films was significantly reduced from 44 to \(33\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure S5, Supporting Information), which can be attributed to the smoother surface of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) when \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) was introduced to the devices.

In order to evaluate the influence of the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) underlayer on light utilization, we measured the optical transmission of the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) film on ITO, as shown in Figure S6 (Supporting Information). Unexpectedly, the lowest transmittance is recorded by bare ITO. After depositing \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), the transmittance successively increases, which is due to the decrease of reflected light.

Uniform and compact perovskite films lay the foundation for efficient PSCs. To better understand the variations in properties of the deposited perovskite films after introducing of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), XRD was performed. Figure 2a shows the XRD patterns of the films based on different ETLs. The strong intensity of the XRD peaks is related to better crystallization. So a better crystal film was obtained which is consistent with SEM results. The optical

absorption spectra of these two films were measured by ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) spectroscopy. Figure 2b shows that the presence of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) does not affect the absorption edge of perovskite films while slightly increasing the absorption intensity. The bandgap of both films extracted from Tauc plots shows an identical value of \(1.53\;\mathrm{eV}\) (Figure S7, Supporting Information).

In order to determine the dynamics of charge transfer and the extraction characteristics of different ETLs, steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectroscopy were performed. As shown in Figure 2c, \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) does not alter the peak luminescence position. The films based on \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) ETL exhibited an emission at 800 and \(799\;\mathrm{nm}\), respectively. Compared with a glass substrate, both films show a strong quenching effect. The PL spectrum of perovskite film on \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) exhibits decreased intensity compared to the film on \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) ETL, which means faster extraction of charges.

The radiative recombination rate from photoinduced carriers competing with nonradiative loss[31] plays a key role in the recombination process. \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) contributes to efficient PSCs by mitigating nonradiative losses. Similar results were confirmed by TRPL. Figure 2d indicates that the PL decay of film based on \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) ETL delivered a longer lifetime than that of film based on \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\). The lifetimes of perovskite film based on glass, \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) ETL are 1794, 528, and 340 ns, respectively.

