The results discussed above reveal that device performance improvement is mainly due to the enhancement of FF and \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\). The hysteresis of the devices was also suppressed by the introduction of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\). Previous studies have demonstrated that hysteresis is mainly caused by ion migration[32] and charge transfer at the interface.[4] In order to further understand the effect of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), the band structures of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) were measured by ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS; Figures S13 and S14, Supporting Information). The optical bandgap values of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) determined by UV–vis absorption spectroscopy are 3.8 and \(3.9~\mathrm{eV}\), respectively (Figure S6a,d, Supporting Information). The calculated conduction band minimum \(\left(E_{\mathrm{CB}}\right)\) is \(-4.55~\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and \(-4.20~\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) (Figure S14, Supporting Information). According to previous studies, the \(E_{\mathrm{CB}}\) of ITO is about \(-4.7~\mathrm{eV}\).[33] The presence of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) with a deeper conduction band matches \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and ITO well, which is beneficial to a higher \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) (Figure 3c). To further elucidate the possible mechanism responsible for the enhancement of \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and FF, capacitance–voltage \((C{-}V)\) measurements were also taken. As shown in Figure 3d, the device based on the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) bilayer has a smaller slope in the linear Mott–Schottky curve and the built-in potentials \(\left(V_{\mathrm{bi}}\right)\) based on \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) are \(\approx915\), 935, and \(985~\mathrm{mV}\), respectively. A higher \(V_{\mathrm{bi}}\) means stronger electric field in the perovskite which is beneficial for enhancing charge transfer,[34] resulting in suppressed hysteresis and more efficient devices.[35]

The performance of PSCs is highly affected by the quality of perovskite films. Excellent contact between interfaces originating from uniform and compact films may be one of the reasons for the higher FF. In order to determine the origin of \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and FF improvements, the trap density of prepared films with different ETLs was evaluated. So, electron-only devices with the ITO/ETL/perovskite/PCBM/bathocuproine (BCP)/Au structure were fabricated. As shown in Figure 3e, the current density reveals an Ohmic-type response at low bias. The cross point is defined as the trap-filled limit voltage \(\left(V_{\mathrm{TFL}}\right)\), when the bias voltage is higher than the \(V_{\mathrm{TFL}}\). The trap density can be determined by \(V_{\mathrm{TFL}}\) using the following equation \(n_{\mathrm{trap}}=2\varepsilon\varepsilon_{0}\) \(V_{\mathrm{TFL}}/e L^{2}\).[31] The trap density is proportional to \(V_{\mathrm{TFL}}\). The results show that the trap density of film deposited on \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) is lower than that of the film deposited on \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\). This is due to low nonradiative recombination and results in a higher \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\). In addition, the dark \(J{-}V\) curves (Figure S15, Supporting Information) of the devices based on \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) indicate that the \(J_{0}\) decreased with the presence of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), which means low leakage current density.

Electrical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) determined the dynamics of charge transfer in PSCs.[36] It is well known that the high-frequency component represents the transfer resistance \(\left(R_{\mathrm{tr}}\right)\) and the low-frequency component indicates the recombination resistance \(\left(R_{\mathrm{re}}\right)\). Small \(R_{\mathrm{tr}}\) is beneficial to electron extraction, and large \(R_{\mathrm{re}}\) means less charge recombination.[37] Figure 3f shows the Nyquist plots of cells with different ETLs under dark conditions, and the equivalent circuit diagram and specific parameters of devices based on \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) are shown in Figure S16 and Table S2 in the Supporting

Information, respectively. The \(R_{\mathrm{re}}\) of devices based on \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) is significantly larger than that of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), meaning low recombination rates and resulting in enhanced FF and \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\).

An optimized device with a PCE of \(23.24\%\) was successfully fabricated (Figure 4a). Significantly, this device demonstrates a high voltage of \(1.17~\mathrm{V}\) with only \(0.36~\mathrm{V}\) loss in potential. This is one of the highest voltages demonstrated by an n–i–p planar structure \((1.53~\mathrm{eV})\). Moreover, the external quantum efficiency (EQE) of the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-based device was measured (Figure 4b). The integrated current density is \(24.34~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}\), matching well with the values from \(J{-}V\) characterization. The steady-state efficiency at maximum power point tracking (MPP) of the optimized PSC was also measured. The maximum voltage for the devices with \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) is \(0.98~\mathrm{V}\), and the maximum current density is \(23.2~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}\). The corresponding steady-state output PCE was \(22.74\%\) (Figure 4c). The best-performing device was sent to a PV calibration laboratory (Quality supervision and Testing Center of Chemical and Physical Power Sources of Information Industry, China) for certification. A certified efficiency of \(22.54\%\) was demonstrated with a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(1.16~\mathrm{V}\), \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(24.78~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}\), and FF of \(78.23\%\) (Figure S17, Supporting Information), making it one of the best planar PSCs. A performance comparison with previous devices based on bilayer ETLs can be found in Table S3 (Supporting Information).

The stability of PSCs is one of the most important criteria for commercialization.[38] The device stability was tested under a nitrogen environment \((25~\mathrm{~\textdegreeC})\) without encapsulation, as shown in Figure 4d. After 80 days, the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-based PSC still maintains \(97.5\%\) of its initial PCE. Compared with \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-based PSCs which retain only \(92.7\%\) of their initial PCE, our results verify the enhanced stability of the new device. Figure 4e shows the normalized PCE changes over time. It is obvious that after \(180~\mathrm{h}\) of continuous illumination, the device based on the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) bilayer maintains \(\approx91\%\) of its initial efficiency. While for the same conditions, the device based on \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) maintains only \(83\%\) of its initial efficiency. The stability was also tested under ambient air (relative humidity (RH) \(75\%\)). After \(120~\mathrm{h}\), the devices based on \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) maintain \(\approx80\%\) of their initial efficiency and the devices based on \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) only provide \(71\%\) of their initial efficiency under the same conditions (Figure 4f). The same conclusion was also confirmed by thermal stability measurements (Figure S18, Supporting Information). The instability of PSCs is due to the decomposition of perovskite materials and organic hole transport layer (HTL). The grain size of perovskite films based on \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) is larger compared to that of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) (Figure 1). The larger grain size of perovskite films may partly suppress the permeation of water at grain boundaries and increase stability. We believe that the stability of perovskite solar cells can be improved further if new HTL materials[39] and/or encapsulation methods[40] are used.

In summary, a simple and effective \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) bilayer ETL was developed for fabricating efficient low-temperature-processed planar PSCs. The presence of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) leads to low trap densities in perovskite films, better energy alignment, and enhanced charge transfer, which result in higher \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and FF. Eventually, an optimized PCE of \(23.24\%\) (certified \(22.54\%\)) was obtained with negligible hysteresis. Moreover, the device based on \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) ETL retains \(97.5\%\) of its initial efficiency after
